{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NEh4YkNhbnpxJNSJN3Gawi","type":"tool_result","content":"Saved E05.1 = no. Next: E05.2 — The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.\n  How to verify: 1) Trace name comparison across servers. 2) Construct repeated confusable substitutions, multiple edits and normalization-boundary examples, starting from an existing tool name; record canonical outputs, comparison result and registration outcome. 3) Answer no if a duplicate or demonstrated lookalike is accepted without a conflict flag. The existence of normalization/edit-distance code is insufficient; namespacing and approval binding do not satisfy this separate check.\nSaved E05.2 = no. Next: E05.3 — Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool.\n  How to verify: 1) Find where tool approval is stored and checked. 2) Check approval is keyed by verified server identity, not tool name or self-reported server name. 3) Answer no if a new server with a matching tool name or server name would count as approved or replace the approved tool.\nSaved E05.3 = no. Next: finish item E05 with `answer.py ... item E05 --rating ...`\nAdded red flag to E05.\nSaved item E05 = gap. Next: E06.1 — Every governed tool path uses an enforced boundary suited to its internal HTTP, public third-party, or local stdio deployment; agent-controlled code cannot bypass it or obtain backend credentials.\n  How to verify: 1) Inventory HTTP, dynamic, CLI/code-mode, wrapper and local-process paths. 2) Trace effective egress, backend credential custody and process/file/env/network restrictions. 3) Internal private networking/mTLS is one option; public services can use agent-side egress and broker-held credentials. 4) Reject any usable unmediated path; do not require operator control of a public provider's network. Use unknown for enfo